{"id":992,"date":"2026-03-18T09:26:23","date_gmt":"2026-03-18T09:26:23","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:10005\/?p=992"},"modified":"2026-08-30T10:50:40","modified_gmt":"2026-03-18T09:26:24","slug":"%e5%9f%ba%e4%ba%8e%e6%8e%92%e5%ba%8f%e7%b4%a2%e5%bc%95%e5%8c%b9%e9%85%8d%e7%9a%84%e9%ab%98%e6%95%88%e6%97%a0%e8%af%af%e5%b7%ae%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e7%94%9f%e6%88%90","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/en\/%e5%9f%ba%e4%ba%8e%e6%8e%92%e5%ba%8f%e7%b4%a2%e5%bc%95%e5%8c%b9%e9%85%8d%e7%9a%84%e9%ab%98%e6%95%88%e6%97%a0%e8%af%af%e5%b7%ae%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e7%94%9f%e6%88%90\/","title":{"rendered":"Efficient Error-Free Secret Key Generation Based on Ranked Index Matching"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3749991f wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"min-height:1000px\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-458a08b5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\"><h2 class=\"lab-paper-title wp-block-post-title\">Efficient Error-Free Secret Key Generation Based on Ranked Index Matching<\/h2>\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">DU Y, DAI H, LIU H, et al.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><em>IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing<\/em><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-text-color has-contrast-3-color has-alpha-channel-opacity has-contrast-3-background-color has-background is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46005010 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udccc Abstract<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote has-text-align-left lab-abstract-quote has-small-font-size\" style=\"margin-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);margin-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0\"><blockquote><p>Secret key generation leveraging the inherent randomness of wireless channels is a key paradigm for physical layer security in wireless networks. However, existing quantization-based methods face difficulties in eliminating noise-induced inconsistent bits. Recent studies have proposed achieving error-free secret key generation by matching segmented channel samples with similar variation patterns between legitimate parties (i.e., channel segments), but such methods also suffer from high computational overhead and degraded matching accuracy when generating long keys. To this end, this paper proposes a sorting-index-based secret key generation method (SIM-SKG) to achieve efficient and error-free key generation. Specifically, by sorting channel samples, each channel segment is ensured to have a unique variation pattern for precise matching; to circumvent the effects of half-duplex communication mode and environmental noise, this paper proposes matching indices rather than channel samples themselves; meanwhile, a noise perturbation scheme is designed to further alleviate ambiguity in the index matching process. Extensive experimental results demonstrate that SIM-SKG achieves high efficiency and accuracy across various scenarios of RSS and CSI channel measurements, completing error-free secret key generation of up to 2048 bits in only 1.7 milliseconds. Furthermore, theoretical analysis and experiments jointly verify the security of SIM-SKG against multiple types of attacks.<\/p><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6adec98e lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udca1 Core Contributions<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<ul start=\"1\" style=\"border-style:none;border-width:0px\" class=\"wp-block-list lab-feature-list\">\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Proposes the SIM-SKG algorithm, enabling negotiation-free error-free secret key generation<\/strong>: By sorting channel samples and matching indices within each segment rather than directly matching channel measurements, the impact of asynchronous sampling caused by half-duplex communication and environmental noise on matching results is effectively reduced, achieving key agreement between legitimate parties without additional interaction.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Designs a multiplicative noise perturbation scheme to improve matching accuracy<\/strong>: To address the issue of repetitive segment patterns after sorting caused by similar channel measurement values, a multiplicative random matrix is introduced to expand the distribution of channel samples, statistically enlarging the gaps between similar measurement values, thereby effectively eliminating ambiguity in segment matching.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Develops a lightweight autonomous error correction mechanism and a re-matching mechanism<\/strong>: To address occasional index inconsistencies caused by noise, an autonomous error correction scheme requiring no information negotiation is designed, avoiding the communication overhead and security risks of multi-round interaction in traditional schemes; meanwhile, a re-matching mechanism is integrated to further enhance the key generation rate.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Conducts comprehensive security analysis, verifying resilience against five types of attacks<\/strong>: Theoretical analysis and experimental validation are conducted for random guessing attacks, inference attacks, imitation attacks, tracking attacks, and reverse attacks, respectively. Results show that the attacker's bit mismatch rate approaches 0.5 (equivalent to random guessing) under all attacks, demonstrating that SIM-SKG achieves information-theoretic security.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-2f9d57f4 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udd27 Method Overview<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-b46d2053 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-18_17-21-25.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-18_17-21-25.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-18_17-21-25.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-18_17-21-25.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-996\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-18_17-21-25\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">The core idea of SIM-SKG is: after sorting channel samples, each channel segment exhibits a unique monotonically increasing or decreasing variation pattern, laying the foundation for precise segment-level matching. The complete process consists of four stages.<strong>Noise perturbation stage<\/strong>, Alice and Bob each multiply their channel samples by the shared random matrix <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u03a0<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\Pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u03a0, broadening the original distribution and enlarging the gaps between similar measurement values.<strong>Sorting and segmentation stage<\/strong>, both parties sort the perturbed channel sequences and divide them into equal-length segments, obtaining their respective segment sequences, each with a unique monotonic variation pattern.<strong>Segment shuffling and index matching stage<\/strong>, Alice shuffles her own segment sequence in a random order <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mi>O<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">RO<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>RO and broadcasts it, while Bob finds the optimal match by comparing the closeness of sorted indices within each segment, thereby both parties agree on the permutation order <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mi>O<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">RO<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>RO without leaking channel measurement values.<strong>Autonomous error correction and privacy amplification stage<\/strong>, a lightweight error correction scheme is used to rectify a small number of index inconsistencies caused by noise, followed by privacy amplification to output the final key. The overall computational complexity is <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>N<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">O(N^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>O(N2), far lower than existing schemes based on bipartite graph matching.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-dda34f26 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcca Experimental Results<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-080de2b0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-18_17-22-45.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-18_17-22-45.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-18_17-22-45.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-18_17-22-45.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-997\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-18_17-22-45\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>SIM-SKG maintains a bit mismatch rate of 0 across all scenarios, while completing error-free secret key generation of 2048 bits in 1.7 milliseconds, with a time overhead of only approximately 1\/40 that of the comparison method DP-SKG<\/strong>: As shown in <strong>Fig. 14<\/strong> As shown, under the same segment length (5) and 256 RSS segments, SIM-SKG generates a 2048-bit key in only 1.7 milliseconds, with time overhead increasing very gently from 0.035 milliseconds to 6.2 milliseconds as key length grows; in contrast, DP-SKG incurs a time overhead exceeding 69 milliseconds, a gap of approximately 40 times.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-e3c2d50d lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcdd Citation<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code lab-bibtex-code has-small-font-size\"><code>@article{du2025simskg,\n  author    = {Yicong Du and Huan Dai and Hongbo Liu and Guyue Li and Yanzhi Ren and Ke Zhang},\n  title     = {Efficient and Error-Free Secret Key Generation Leveraging Sorted Indices Matching},\n  journal   = {IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing},\n  volume    = {24},\n  number    = {2},\n  pages     = {779--793},\n  month     = feb,\n  year      = {2025},\n  doi       = {10.1109\/TMC.2024.3465042}\n}\n<\/code><\/pre>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div style=\"height:100px\" aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-spacer\"><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-content-justification-space-between is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-788841da wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Secret key generation leveraging the inherent randomness of wireless channels is a key paradigm for physical layer security in wireless networks. However, existing quantization-based methods face difficulties in eliminating inconsistent bits caused by noise. Recent research has proposed achieving error-free secret key generation by matching segmented channel samples with similar variation patterns between legitimate parties (i.e., channel segments).<\/p>","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":996,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"mypost","format":"standard","meta":{"_uag_custom_page_level_css":"","footnotes":""},"categories":[5],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-992","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-research"],"uagb_featured_image_src":{"full":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io